# guard-b allowlist for donmai-smokes — same grammar as the engine's own
# header (scripts/guard-b-lint.sh), three or four ' :: '-separated fields:
#
#     <RULE_ID>[,<RULE_ID>...] :: <location-regex> :: <match-regex>[ :: <line-regex>]
#
# match-regex binds THE TEXT THE RULE MATCHED, not the surrounding line — see
# scripts/guard-b-lint.sh's header for why. Where a file names a banned token
# more than once for different reasons, the location-regex pins the exact
# line number instead, which is a tighter bind than a line-regex would be.
#
# NOTE ON THE [BRACKETS]: this file is itself scanned, so every entry below
# spells the banned literal with one bracketed letter (`Rens[e]i`, `/api/cl[i]/`)
# — the same convention scripts/guard-b-lint.sh and its own .guard-allowlist
# use. This changes nothing about what is matched at scan time.

# --- Parent-brand / boundary description in the harness + smoke-plan docs ---
# This repo's entire reason for existing is "exercise the OSS half of the
# donmai <-> closed-platform boundary" (see AGENTS.md, README.md). Describing
# that boundary necessarily names the parent product on the other side of it.
# Pinned to the four exact lines that do so, not a blanket file exemption.
BRAND_NAME :: ^README\.md:5$ :: Rens[e]i
BRAND_NAME :: ^README\.md:11$ :: Rens[e]i
BRAND_NAME :: ^README\.md:78$ :: Rens[e]i
BRAND_NAME :: ^harness/doc\.go:6$ :: Rens[e]i

# --- "This repo does NOT call the closed CLI namespace" boundary guardrails -
# AGENTS.md and README.md state the platform-free contract; the four test
# files restate it as an in-file comment banner. All of these NAME the closed
# route prefix in order to forbid it, exactly the case
# donmai-architecture/.guard-allowlist carves out for its own AGENTS.md.
CLOSED_CLI_ENDPOINT :: ^AGENTS\.md:88$ :: /api/cl[i]/
CLOSED_CLI_ENDPOINT :: ^README\.md:9$ :: /api/cl[i]/
CLOSED_CLI_ENDPOINT :: ^step11_hardfail_ux_test\.go:27$ :: /api/cl[i]/
CLOSED_CLI_ENDPOINT :: ^step16_arch_assess_native_test\.go:29$ :: /api/cl[i]/
CLOSED_CLI_ENDPOINT :: ^step17_sibling_context_repos_test\.go:40$ :: /api/cl[i]/

# --- "This repo does NOT read the closed test-token env var" guardrail ------
# Same shape: AGENTS.md names the var to forbid its use, not to use it.
CLOSED_ENV_VAR :: ^AGENTS\.md:91$ :: RENS[E]I_TEST_TOKEN

# --- In-package boundary self-guard's own fragment-assembled token table ----
# interactive/boundary_test.go is itself a leak guard (TestPlatformFreeBoundary,
# scoped to the interactive/ package), predating this repo-wide one — see its
# own header comment. Its token table assembles each banned string from
# fragments so the file declaring the guard doesn't trip it, except the
# Linear-host entry, which only splits off the "api." prefix and leaves
# "linear.app/graphql" whole in one string literal. That is test data for a
# complementary guard, not a leak: pinned to the exact line.
TRACKER_URL :: ^interactive/boundary_test\.go:26$ :: l[i]near\.app/graphql
